# git — recover from a detached HEAD

`git status` reports:

HEAD detached at <SHA>

You're not on any branch. New commits won't belong to any branch; they'd be lost when HEAD moves away unless rescued.

app :git__recover_from_a_detached_head do
  title       "git — recover from a detached HEAD"
  description "`git status` reports: HEAD detached at <SHA> You're not on any branch."
end

## The recovery decision

Three sub-cases:

1. *Detached but no new commits yet* → just check out a real
     branch. Nothing to lose.
2. *Detached and you've made commits you want to keep* →
     create a new branch from the current HEAD, then check out wherever you were going.
3. *Detached and the new commits should NOT be kept* → check
     out a branch; the commits become unreachable and will be garbage-collected after ~30 days (`git reflog` recovers them in the meantime if you change your mind).

## Workflow

### 1. confirm detached + see if commits exist
```bash
  git status
  git log --oneline HEAD --not --all | head -10
```

If the second command output is empty, you have no unreachable commits → case 1, safe to switch.

### 2a. case 1 — just switch back to a branch
```bash
  git checkout main          # or whatever branch you meant to be on
```

### 2b. case 2 — save the detached commits to a new branch first
```bash
  git branch rescue-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M)   # mark the current HEAD with a branch
  git checkout main                         # now safe to switch
  # rescue-... contains your work; merge or cherry-pick from it later
```

## Gotchas

- Do NOT `git reset --hard` when in detached state — it just
moves HEAD to where you already are; doesn't help.
- Do NOT delete the rescue branch until you've actually used
or rejected its commits (`git branch -d rescue-...` refuses if unmerged; use `-D` to force-delete only when sure).
- `git reflog` shows the last ~30 days of HEAD movements —
can resurrect a "lost" commit even after the rescue branch is gone.

